fix(build): give every Robolectric module a 2g test-worker heap

CI's `Run unit tests` step failed on two consecutive commits while the
release APK built fine, and it failed the same way each time:

  Execution failed for task ':feature:browse:testDebugUnitTest'.
  java.nio.file.NoSuchFileException: ...\binary\in-progress-results-generic.bin

That exception is the symptom, not the cause. The results file is absent
because the test worker JVM was killed before it could flush its results,
so no assertion ever failed. Gradle then reports the missing file, which
reads like corrupt output rather than an exhausted worker.

The cause is an asymmetry in our own build config. `:app` sets
`maxHeapSize = "2g"`; `:feature:browse`, `:feature:reader` and `:core:data`
all enable `isIncludeAndroidResources = true` for Robolectric and set no
heap at all, leaving them on Gradle's 512m default while running Compose
UI tests. The limit was added where the failure first appeared, not where
it applies.

All three modules now carry the same 2g heap as `:app`, and each comment
records why, so the next module to enable Robolectric inherits the
reasoning rather than rediscovering it.

This also explains why the failure was inconsistent locally. `:feature:
browse` passes when run alone and fails inside the full sweep, because
peak memory depends on what else is resident at the time \u2014 so it reads
as a flaky test rather than a deterministic misconfiguration.

Verified with the exact command CI runs (`testDebugUnitTest` across all
modules, then XML aggregation): BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 5m 55s with 873 tasks
genuinely re-executed and 1,880 tests, 0 failures across 8 modules. The
previous local runs were misleading \u2014 they reported BUILD SUCCESSFUL while
695 to 708 tasks were UP-TO-DATE, so the tests had not actually re-run.

No production code touched; no behaviour change.